unfortunetly for you i just came up with some devious shit: two independent games can ask for the same leaf node to be evaluated - we wish to avoid duplicate computation. thats why we maintain 8192 (since it should be memory cheap to store game slots, the expensive part is the node stack, which doesnt grow much with this number) games at the same time. each has a sim counter, which steps up once for every mcts simulation (leaf evaluated) it performs. so whevener we encounter two games asking for the same leaf, we dont add the same leaf again, instead we do one more games simulation, from the extra games we have. now this introduces the issiue that all the games will not step in lockstep, as some games might be on sim 201 while others are on sim 50. the mcts worker maintains a number for games ready to step, and when that number reaches 2048 or more (theoretically this number could be anything, as its just a heuristic that balances memory usage with time spent on stepping) we step every aplicable game, and do a memory clear as described. the memory clear doesnt impact games in the middle of their mcts search, since all their nodes are reachable by definitiion, and only clears the nodes of the games stepped now. however with such a complex node clear system a question arrises for wether we will leak memory. for that, at each node, track an int for how many times its been seen during a step (every step involves scanning over every node in the stack), for now we dont do anything with this int, its just a signal for wether there are nodes that dont get properly cleared.
whenever a game finishes, we slot in a new fresh game in its place. because for a game to finish it requres a step, and that step will clear any of that games nodes, we should be fine, but just confirm that that is what happens, and that when a game starts it starts with no nodes stored for it.
because inter game node sharing is assumed to be small, its also assumed that only about 2200 games of the 8192 slots will ever be touched, but we do that just in case as it doesnt cost much, and in the exceedingly rare case that a single pass uses all of them, we just pack the remaining spots in the model buffer empty and log it somewhere that shit has gone down.
for each game node we store its zobrist hash, we index it by that, also duing search, we use it to check if a child node exists or if its a leaf.
this way, while we have 2000+ games, we only have one singular unordered stack of nodes, all with their own game instance and mcts stats.
we also want tree reuse. so when a game steps, we do not want to start the mcts search from scratch, rarther reuse the nodes we already computed, and only discard the impossible ones. the solution for that is a reachability check. however checking all nodes against all boards is too expensive. thats why we introduce a system of game id. each game is numbered 0 through 2047, each node holds 16 16bit game id slots. we search games untill leaves sequentially, so whenever we are on game n, we remember n, and write that into every node that we encounter in that search. this assumes that inter game node sharing is less than 16 at most, and we will benchmark that, but its a good starting point. inter game reuse is assumed to be miniscule tho. then at the end of the search, when the entire mcts makes a step, we remove and keep nodes based on teh policy of checking their reachability against the new game state of only the games present in the game ids.
its important that roots are never allowed to fuse and in the case that two games step to the same root they are split up. roots are always separate from the node stack in order to make sure noise or random exploration is allowed to be different for them.
this idea of sharing nodes works because visit counts on nodes do not contaminate the root counts, and more exploration only increases the signal quality.
either way inter game sharing will be exceedingly rare, and intra game node sharing will dominate.
what this all achieves is that every single model call includes only unique positions, and every evaluated position is used to its maximum by mcts.
